It often arrives at night. The baby is finally asleep, the house is quiet, and a thought shows up that sounds very reasonable: other mothers manage this, so why can't I?

If that thought has been keeping you company, I want to say something clearly. Needing help does not mean you are failing. It means you are going through one of the biggest transitions of your life, and you deserve care while you do it.

## Where the guilt comes from

Most of us grew up with a picture of the mother who does it all. She recovers fast, she knows what every cry means, and she never needs anyone. Nobody tells you that picture was never real.

What is real is this. Your body just did something enormous. You are healing, feeding, getting to know a brand new person and sleeping in pieces. Needing support in the middle of all that is not a flaw in you. It is an honest response to what is actually happening.

Many of us also feel that help has to be earned. As if we need to be falling apart first, or prove we tried hard enough alone. You do not have to earn it. You are allowed to need things before it gets that far.

And if what you are feeling is heavier than guilt, if it feels dark or it does not lift, please tell your OB, midwife or doctor. That is asking for help too, and it is one of the bravest kinds.

## What asking can look like

Asking does not have to be a big conversation. Sometimes it is one sentence.

"Can you hold the baby while I shower?"

"Can you bring dinner on Thursday?"

"Can you take the baby for the first part of the night so I can sleep?"

Sometimes it is saying yes when someone offers, instead of "we're fine." And sometimes it is reaching outside the family, because the people who love you most are also tired, or far away, or not sure what to do.

Help looks different in every home. For one mother it is a hot meal and a clean kitchen. For another it is someone who can answer a feeding question without judgment. For another it is simply a calm adult in the room at two in the morning. None of these needs is smaller than the others.

## What my third postpartum taught me

I have been through postpartum three times, and each season was different. My third was the hardest. I remember searching for support and answers in the middle of the night, feeling like everyone else had figured out something I had missed.

Reaching out did not make me less of a mother. It helped me find my way through. That experience is a big part of why Cora exists.

I also think about my own mother. She went through her postpartum without a support network, and she still found ways to care for other women after birth. She was strong. She also deserved not to do it alone.

Strength and support are not opposites. You can be capable and still need a hand. Some of the strongest mothers I have met are the ones who finally let someone in.

So if you are reading this with one hand, in the dark, here is what I hope you keep. You are not behind. You are not doing it wrong. You are a person who is healing and learning at the same time, and that is a lot to hold.

Needing help is not a sign that something is wrong with you. It is a sign that you are paying attention.
